1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
AI can give a child a confident answer in seconds.
Did they learn to think with it—or accept it?

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Before You Believe It turns a weekend AI moment
into a small parent-and-child practice.

3
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:36,000
The child chooses what makes them pause.
That question—not the model—sets the direction.

4
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Push Back names the hidden assumption
inside a confident AI-style claim.

5
00:00:48,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Check connects a clue or another view—not a verdict—
and keeps uncertainty visible.

6
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Make and Own keep the child’s working answer local.
The Learning Receipt separates contributions.

7
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:24,000
The activity has no points, answer key, or model verdict.
Parents can see the reasoning moves; children lead the question.

8
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Recording pauses here while the real GPT-5.6 request runs.
Generation time is never faked.

9
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:46,000
GPT-5.6 returns a claim, assumption, clues,
parent prompt, and an uncertainty to explore.

10
00:01:46,000 --> 00:02:00,000
It never grades, diagnoses, ranks, recommends,
or decides what a child should believe.

11
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Codex helped build and test the canvas.
Signed access and schema validation protect the GPT-5.6 boundary.

12
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:20,592
AI prepares material to question.
The family does the reasoning.
